WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.150
Film Courage: What are some of the first things
an actor has to do when they get to Los Angeles?

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:07.029
Bill Oberst, Jr.: Realize that you are going
to be rejected a lot.

00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:10.280
It will take longer than you ever thought.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:15.309
I came with a two year plan and I thought
that I was giving myself a lot of extra time.

00:00:15.309 --> 00:00:21.640
But I’ve been here four full years (this
is going into my fifth year).

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:25.670
Only in the last year did I have any kind
of decent success.

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:29.730
And by that I mean that I can put food on
the table regularly.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:36.350
But I think if you come you have to decide
am I going to cut all ties with other careers

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:38.640
and do this or not?

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.730
And I chose to not have a day job, which made
it more difficult but I couldn’t have reached

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:45.020
any kind of success without it.

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:49.080
Film Courage: When you came here four years
ago, how many IMDB credits did you have?

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.080
Bill Oberst, Jr.: Two.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:51.340
Film Courage: And how many do you have now?

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:52.340
Bill Oberst, Jr.: One hundred.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:56.170
I just passed my one hundredth IMDB credit
and still nobody knows who the hell I am.

00:00:56.170 --> 00:01:02.340
So you can make a lot of movies without actually
bursting in to the big public consciousness.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:05.100
I think that’s another thing actors need
to understand.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.460
You know, I’ve done a couple of TV movies
for the Hallmark Channel and they’re great

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.530
and I’m glad to be in them and yes, they
help.

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:14.310
But they do not change your life and they
don’t change your career.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:18.369
There is so much noise that an actor has to
burst through that you have to understand

00:01:18.369 --> 00:01:21.990
even if you get a big project, it may be a
bump (but it’s probably going to be a slight

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:22.990
bump).

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:26.570
It’s more the accumulation of IMDB credits
that make a difference.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:29.439
Film Courage: What about getting acclimated
to LA.

00:01:29.439 --> 00:01:32.909
You came from somewhere else?

00:01:32.909 --> 00:01:35.950
How does one get acclimated to LA and actor’s
hours?

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:39.619
Bill Oberst, Jr.: Well I came from the East
Coast.

00:01:39.619 --> 00:01:43.109
I’d done theater in New York and up and
down the East Coast for 14 years.

00:01:43.109 --> 00:01:45.672
And I came out here expecting it to be much
the same and it’s not.

00:01:45.672 --> 00:01:47.719
LA is not really a city.

00:01:47.719 --> 00:01:49.590
That is one thing you have to understand if
you come here.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:50.960
It’s a collection of smaller cities.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.079
The geography is much different.

00:01:54.079 --> 00:02:02.740
And your friendships, your ability to do work
is all determined by traffic.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:07.299
And that’s a huge thing you’ll realize
when you come here.

00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:12.740
If you come to LA to be an actor, you have
to be completely available emotionally, spiritually,

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:13.780
physically.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:17.500
Your relationships (whatever they are) have
to understand that this is your priority.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:24.500
Michael Caine said, and I think he’s right,
he said “If you want to make it, this can

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:26.690
not be just a full time job.

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:28.370
It has to be a full time obsession.”

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:31.330
And I find that to be very true.

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:35.390
You have to be completely obsessed to the
point where people who are not in the business

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:39.530
will say “You’re crazy!”

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:43.590
If they don’t say that, you’re probably
not obsessed enough to make it in the business.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:50.450
Film Courage: What about looking for that
side job while you are looking for those acting

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:52.870
roles that are going to put food on the table?

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:57.170
Where are you looking for those side jobs
and what types of hours would you look for

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:58.170
in terms of those jobs?

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:01.840
Bill Oberst, Jr.: If you think you have to
have a side job when you come to LA, try to

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.080
get one that is very, very flexible.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:08.220
Many people are bartenders, caterers, anything
that is part-time.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:13.300
Don’t get something that is going to lock
you in particular hours every week because

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:17.320
invariably you’ll miss an audition and that’s
not going to do anything…you might not have

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:19.750
gotten the audition, but it kills your morale.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:22.940
So I came here with a no-day-job plan and
it’s been tough.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:25.060
There have been a lot of peanut butter years.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:28.090
It’s a good thing that I am thin.

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:31.490
That’s another thing about coming to LA.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:34.720
You should probably decide that you’re not
going to eat as much that you thought you

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:37.910
might eat or that you’re accustomed to eating
in the first few years.

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:40.970
You’ll have times when you eat nothing but
noodles and peanut butter.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:43.340
It’s just the nature of the business.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:45.120
Other times you’ll be eating steak.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.140
But your day job should be flexible.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:51.670
Film Courage: And what do you wish someone
told you before you got to LA?

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:52.670
Or are you glad no one said anything?

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:56.720
Bill Oberst, Jr.: I wish that before I had
come someone said “Don’t come because

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:59.060
this is very, very difficult.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:02.580
It’s a cruel town and a cruel business.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:04.820
No one cares and you have to do it yourself.”

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:06.510
Now, I still would have come!

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:11.770
But I would have come with more awareness
of just how difficult this business is.

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:19.539
All that people ever see is the gloss, the
entertainment and the glamour.

00:04:19.539 --> 00:04:22.860
That’s all produced for the general public,
not for actors.

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:28.470
I wish that someone would produce content
for actors saying “Do-not-move-to-Los-Angeles,

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:33.110
it is very, very difficult.”

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:38.280
And then the only people who will do it are
those who say, by damn, I am going to do it.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:39.280
I can take it.

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:41.199
Because that’s the kind of spirit you have
to have.

00:04:41.199 --> 00:04:46.419
You have to be tenacious and you can never,
ever give up or you will not make it.

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:49.639
Film Courage: I think one thing that is interesting
is that when people don’t have somewhere

00:04:49.639 --> 00:04:52.360
to go back to, they tend to stick it out.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:57.090
What would you tell someone on how to emotionally
survive coming here?

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:58.090
Because it is.

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:01.129
It’s very cold and I think people need an
outlet whether it’s reading, hiking, their

00:05:01.129 --> 00:05:02.129
church, whatever it is.

00:05:02.129 --> 00:05:03.930
Bill Oberst, Jr.: I hike in Griffith Park.

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:05.960
I come to this place [gestures to the beautiful
Hollywood Presbyterian church around him that

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:08.460
we’re shooting at] every Sunday morning.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.020
And I spend time here This is my solace.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:13.330
I think you have to have something outside
of yourself, a relationship with God, a higher

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:17.210
power, your relationships with your family.

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:23.689
You have to have other outlets other than
acting because it allows you to put your passion

00:05:23.689 --> 00:05:24.740
into your acting.

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:30.229
At the same time, I know people who never
think about acting except when they have an

00:05:30.229 --> 00:05:31.229
audition.

00:05:31.229 --> 00:05:32.270
I think that’s a big mistake.

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:36.430
You’re a small business person and you have
to think of yourself as a small business person.

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:38.499
So when I get up in the morning, I am going
to work.

00:05:38.499 --> 00:05:42.340
I put my feet on the floor, I am at work because
I am my business.

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:46.580
So there is not a moment that goes by, even
if I am hiking, even if I am reading, I’m

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:50.439
always looking for things that I can apply
to my business.

00:05:50.439 --> 00:05:55.480
We’re entrepreneurs and that’s another
thing that people don’t say to actors.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:06:00.169
They say “Come here and explore your inner
self and feel the character!”

00:06:00.169 --> 00:06:01.370
Well yeah, that’s great.

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:03.280
But first you have to get on set.

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:06.040
And you don’t do that unless you’re a
good business person.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:08.559
I don’t mean to sound so earnest.

00:06:08.559 --> 00:06:10.979
This is what LA has done to me.

00:06:10.979 --> 00:06:14.439
This is what LA will do to an actor if you
achieve any type of success here.

00:06:14.439 --> 00:06:18.460
You become a bit more serious and people say
“Boy, you’re kind of a hard a$$.”

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:19.919
But it’s a tough town.

